From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 23:49:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] log: write the log file from its own thread

Every thread that logs wrote its line to the log file itself, under the
lock of the sink all loggers share, and flushed it: flush_on(debug) makes
each line a write() into the file. A write normally only copies into the
page cache, but on a filesystem whose journal is committing to storage
that is slow to answer it waits, and every thread that logs waits behind
it on the shared lock.

With debug logging on, the decoder, the renderer and the audio thread of
a playing video log several lines per frame. On a box whose storage is a
USB stick, one such write took 140 ms: all of them stopped for that long
together, two late frames were dropped and the picture was 126 ms off the
audio until the clock caught up.

The file sink now only formats the line on the thread that logs and
queues it; a writer thread of its own appends and flushes what is queued.
Lines keep the time and thread they were logged with and their order.
Nothing is dropped: past 8 MiB queued (about a minute of debug logging)
the thread that logs waits for the writer. Destroying the sink, as
Deinitialize does, writes out the queue before it returns.

What is still queued when Kodi crashes is lost; the writer takes the
queue as soon as a line arrives, so that is the last few milliseconds.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

diff --git a/xbmc/utils/AsyncFileSink.cpp b/xbmc/utils/AsyncFileSink.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9238759
--- /dev/null
+++ b/xbmc/utils/AsyncFileSink.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,82 @@
+/*
